CME ORCHESTRATE - FREEBSD INSTALLATION
=====================================

Project: https://github.com/misterhelbig/acme-orchestrate

This package installs the ACME orchestration scripts, DNS plugin,
sample configurations, documentation and newsyslog configuration.

It does not configure DNS credentials, certificate definitions, cron,
SSH authentication or production certificate state.

Prerequisites
-------------
- FreeBSD 15.
- acme.sh, bind-tools and rsync (installed as package dependencies).
- A dedicated acme user and group, normally created by the FreeBSD
  acme.sh package.
- An authoritative DNS zone accepting authenticated RFC 2136 updates.
- A TSIG key authorised to update the challenge zone.
- Public ACME DNS-01 CNAME delegation for each managed DNS name.
- Dedicated local or remote directories for certificate deployment.
- SSH authentication where remote replicas are configured.

Installation
------------
Install the downloaded FreeBSD package as root:

  pkg install ./acme-orchestrate-<version>.pkg

Verify:

  pkg info acme-orchestrate
  pkg check -s acme-orchestrate
  id acme

The acme user must have write access to its state and account files.

Production state is maintained under:

  /var/db/acme/
  /var/db/acme/.acme.sh/
  /var/db/acme/certs/
  /var/db/acme/deployment/

Ensure the state directory and certificate home exist with suitable
ownership and permissions before the first reconciliation.

For example, as root:

  install -d -o acme -g acme -m 700 /var/db/acme
  install -d -o acme -g acme -m 700 /var/db/acme/.acme.sh
  install -d -o acme -g acme -m 700 /var/db/acme/certs

The orchestrator maintains its own deployment snapshots and inventory.

Certificate configuration
-------------------------
Copy the packaged example:

  /usr/local/etc/acme/domains.conf.sample

to:

  /usr/local/etc/acme/domains.conf

Edit the file to describe certificate bundles and replica sets.

Configuration format:

  $alias = destination

  parent-domain: challenge-alias: replicas: apex: SANs

Example:

  $web = root@webserver:/usr/local/etc/ssl/acme/
  $local = /usr/local/etc/ssl/acme/

  bar.net: bar-net: $web,$local: apex: *
  bar.net: vpn-bar-net: $local: none: vpn

The first definition requests one certificate for bar.net and
*.bar.net, and assigns it to both replica sets.

The second requests a certificate for vpn.bar.net.

Each challenge alias must be unique. Every certificate must reference
at least one defined replica alias.

Replica destinations must be dedicated directories. The push command
uses rsync --delete and may remove files not present in its snapshot.

Challenge zone configuration
----------------------------
The package installs:

  /usr/local/etc/acme/orchestrate.conf.sample

On a fresh installation, the FreeBSD package @sample mechanism also
creates:

  /usr/local/etc/acme/orchestrate.conf

Configure the authoritative DNS challenge zone:

  ACME_CHALLENGE_ZONE='acme.example.net'

Replace the example domain with the actual authoritative zone used
for delegated ACME DNS-01 challenges.

This setting controls the DNS challenge aliases constructed and
validated by acme-orchestrate.

IMPORTANT: The DNS plugin independently uses NSUPDATE_ZONE.

  ACME_CHALLENGE_ZONE and NSUPDATE_ZONE must identify the same zone.

Changing orchestrate.conf does not automatically update the plugin's
saved account configuration.

DNS plugin installation
-----------------------
The package installs the plugin at:

  /usr/local/share/acme-orchestrate/dns_nsupdate.sh

It must be accessible from the dnsapi directory in the acme.sh home
used by the orchestrator.

For example:

  /var/db/acme/.acme.sh/dnsapi/dns_nsupdate.sh

A symbolic link to the packaged copy is recommended so that future
package upgrades also update the active plugin.

As root, on a fresh installation:

  install -d -o acme -g acme -m 700 /var/db/acme/.acme.sh/dnsapi

  ln -s /usr/local/share/acme-orchestrate/dns_nsupdate.sh 
    /var/db/acme/.acme.sh/dnsapi/dns_nsupdate.sh

Check for an existing file or link before creating this link on an
established installation.

The staging acme.sh home must also have access to the plugin when
staging issuance is required.

Initial nsupdate configuration
------------------------------
The plugin uses these environment variables:

  NSUPDATE_SERVER       Authoritative DNS server address(es)
  NSUPDATE_SERVER_PORT  DNS update port (normally 53)
  NSUPDATE_KEY          Absolute path to the TSIG key file
  NSUPDATE_ZONE         Authoritative zone to update
  NSUPDATE_OPT          Additional nsupdate options (optional)

Example for an acme user using tcsh:

  setenv NSUPDATE_SERVER 192.0.2.1
  setenv NSUPDATE_SERVER_PORT 53
  setenv NSUPDATE_KEY /var/db/acme/.acme.sh/acme-update.key
  setenv NSUPDATE_ZONE acme.example.net
  setenv NSUPDATE_OPT ""

192.0.2.1 and acme.example.net are documentation examples.

The TSIG key file must be provisioned independently. It must be
readable by acme and protected against access by other users.

The DNS server must be configured to authorise updates using the
corresponding TSIG key.

These NSUPDATE_* environment variables are required when the plugin
first processes a DNS challenge.

The plugin persists them using acme.sh's account configuration,
saving entries named:

  SAVED_NSUPDATE_SERVER
  SAVED_NSUPDATE_SERVER_PORT
  SAVED_NSUPDATE_KEY
  SAVED_NSUPDATE_ZONE
  SAVED_NSUPDATE_OPT

Future invocations normally retrieve the persisted values. The
environment variables are not required in scheduled cron jobs.

Production and staging have separate acme.sh account configurations.
They therefore require separate initialisation.

A dry-run does not initialise the DNS plugin, because it does not
perform live DNS updates.

Do not publish TSIG key contents or ACME account credentials.

DNS delegation
--------------
Each managed certificate name requires public DNS-01 delegation into
the configured challenge zone.

For example:

  _acme-challenge.bar.net. IN CNAME _acme-challenge.bar-net.acme.example.net.

The challenge alias "bar-net" must agree with domains.conf.

For a wildcard certificate covering bar.net and *.bar.net, the
same _acme-challenge.bar.net name is used for DNS-01 validation.

Verify the CNAME before issuing:

  dig _acme-challenge.bar.net CNAME +short

The authoritative challenge zone must make dynamically created TXT
records publicly resolvable.

Initial reconciliation
----------------------
Run as the acme user:

  /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate --dry-run

Review certificate changes, SANs, planned replica snapshots and
housekeeping actions.

After confirming configuration and DNS delegation, perform a live run
with the initial NSUPDATE_* environment variables available:

  /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate

Verify successful certificate issuance and snapshot publication.

After the first DNS challenge, check that SAVED_NSUPDATE_* entries
were written to the applicable acme.sh account.conf.

Use the staging environment for controlled issuance tests where
appropriate.

Do not enable scheduled jobs until initial configuration is validated.

Deployment
----------
Run as root.

Preview:

  /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate-push --dry-run

Deploy:

  /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate-push

The push command uses rsync to synchronise prepared snapshots to
configured local or remote destinations.

Each destination must already exist and must be exclusively managed
by this software.

The command uses rsync --delete.

Missing destination directories require operator intervention.
Historical destinations removed from configuration are not
automatically cleaned up.

The consuming application may need to be reloaded to use new
certificates. This is not performed by the package.

Cron
----
Install these entries in the indicated crontabs.

acme user's crontab:

  12 3 * * 1,4 /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate-runner

root's crontab:

  12 4 * * 1,4 /usr/local/sbin/acme-orchestrate-push >> /var/log/acme-orchestrate-push.log 2>&1

These run Mondays and Thursdays at 03:12 and 04:12 using the system's
local timezone.

The jobs are independent. Certificate reconciliation is performed
first; deployment is scheduled separately.

Do not schedule acme-orchestrate directly. The runner provides the
dry-run preflight, locking and logging.

Logging
-------
The package installs:

  /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/acme-orchestrate.conf.sample

The @sample mechanism creates the active configuration:

  /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d/acme-orchestrate.conf

Two logs are managed:

  /var/log/acme-orchestrate.log
  /var/log/acme-orchestrate-push.log

The supplied configuration uses size-based rotation at 10 MiB,
retains ten compressed archives and supports log file creation.

Check without rotating:

  newsyslog -nvv

Create missing configured logs:

  newsyslog -CN

acme.sh has its own separate newsyslog configuration.

Manual operation
----------------
As acme:

  acme-orchestrate --dry-run
  acme-orchestrate
  acme-orchestrate --staging --dry-run

As root:

  acme-orchestrate-push --dry-run
  acme-orchestrate-push

Review all proposed changes before live operations.

Security and backups
--------------------
Do not import production private keys, certificates, TSIG secrets,
ACME account credentials or deployment state into the Git repository
or published package.

Back up operational state and credentials separately using
appropriate ownership, permissions and storage protection.

Do not configure independent production orchestrators without
coordinating issuance, renewal schedules and certificate state.

